#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/assets/0a034d98da82572a523d406379ab286d1af6b37820f5c2380822abf611643f83.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/assets/89189584fab0cbb79fb0b4ac30a81b41bfd6508de7499c111fd53fb6c0c2c928.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/assets/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822b1894-de17-5a39-b99c-38ebb8d1cf37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316047} {:answer/id #uuid "39875807-fe18-587b-8863-7346df9ccb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316048} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb8dbd8-ce6e-5f16-8b79-77be55ddeb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316049} {:answer/id #uuid "610741b1-d059-5614-beb3-7a8eec8ec8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316050} {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6998-7559-5bed-9ec2-a1771dd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6998-7559-5bed-9ec2-a1771dd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316051} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf90ac7-69d3-5fff-bf5a-ddb95afa0f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27d39cb2-4afd-51aa-ba9c-4ddc1b6ff266" :question/math-academy-id "q-130868" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12456346-b6a7-544d-ad76-1a777b9829e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ an $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316054} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e5bd9f-669b-5474-80ef-344d221820e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} {:answer/id #uuid "0f27e316-616c-5c96-aaea-3590c5a7f34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316056} {:answer/id #uuid "4afb4ada-2856-55cf-b68b-200a737f4bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316057} {:answer/id #uuid "2f438f20-12bf-5efc-9021-7b6f0bb59358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f438f20-12bf-5efc-9021-7b6f0bb59358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316058} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd8dd10c-0982-5496-9bfc-972d9275245b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d6c8932-9459-5228-9f76-3c00a801677b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131347" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d92c6ef3-dc52-59e4-8daf-26b8990eb382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386102} {:answer/id #uuid "d856643a-37b8-5ad5-863e-75fd769576bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386103} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa31754-e114-5f9f-a0f7-26638c423de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386104} {:answer/id #uuid "23af7e0e-3b7a-5ab1-83c7-0ded63a2a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386105} {:answer/id #uuid "e213c815-52cb-58e1-bfd8-d4b3b0067b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23af7e0e-3b7a-5ab1-83c7-0ded63a2a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386105} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1bedaad-9c36-5e3f-bfc9-8bb03bf0ddab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69c3d843-e9dd-52a0-9c3a-7112e0ce14b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131380" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1434626-7592-55e2-8c20-d8e0763453d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316061} {:answer/id #uuid "f220a910-1c8f-5ff5-9795-f881b26cc0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316062} {:answer/id #uuid "2620eb0b-038f-5a5b-bc41-af20438232ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316063} {:answer/id #uuid "61a942d0-3f57-51be-934b-23a1d6f6f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316064} {:answer/id #uuid "367cdc21-d31c-5c70-99dd-6a04769ac3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61a942d0-3f57-51be-934b-23a1d6f6f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316064} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd019b54-0c3a-52c1-961b-b6d468db5ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a618d522-1f89-5278-907e-8cb0fd49bf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-131381" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6529732-9b49-5f7a-ae84-2ee74ff49a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386109} {:answer/id #uuid "723f1cdd-d867-5391-96a4-6cfe5c90348d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386110} {:answer/id #uuid "402333e9-bffc-5f6a-b40f-70a6f7f92759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386111} {:answer/id #uuid "4b816960-cd52-53da-a63b-345c4979c8b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386112} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cc9629-0092-53a7-bbab-0a2c79ac7d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6529732-9b49-5f7a-ae84-2ee74ff49a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386109} :answer-field/id #uuid "717058eb-3183-5cd1-bdd6-6107f160ff09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "784f0439-5a9c-50b5-bcf3-3949291e16a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-131384" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${E}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $E$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${E}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58574ec8-399a-5f6e-b6f2-d2f9038956ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316082} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5e7730-2ce7-5404-a715-71e79b625181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316083} {:answer/id #uuid "47ad9c3b-7721-57b0-9963-ebd1e32af9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316084} {:answer/id #uuid "da5b93c8-c6e6-57ab-8524-d917ba5b90fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316085} {:answer/id #uuid "ad716d96-451e-55ce-9a8c-2f9fafbe2b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} 